 To create a shallow clone of an object in JavaScript, you can use the `Object.assign()` method. This method copies the values of all enumerable own properties from one or more source objects to a target object. Here's a simple function that takes an object as an argument and returns a shallow copy of it:

```javascript
function shallowClone(obj) {
  if (typeof obj !== 'object' || obj === null) {
    throw new Error('Input must be an object');
  }

  return Object.assign({}, obj);
}

// Usage example:
const original = { a: 1, b: { c: 2 } };
const clone = shallowClone(original);

console.log(clone); // { a: 1, b: { c: 2 } }
console.log(clone === original); // false
console.log(clone.b === original.b); // true
```

This function checks if the input is an object or null, and if not, it throws an error. Then it creates a new empty object and uses `Object.assign()` to copy the properties from the given object to the new one.

Please note that this function creates a shallow copy, meaning that it only copies the properties of the object and their values. If the properties are objects themselves, the nested objects will reference the same memory location as in the original object. If you need a deep clone, you should use a library like lodash or a custom recursive function.

