 To create a Perl script that parses a given string and extracts all names, we can use regular expressions (regex) to match patterns that resemble common name formats. Here's a simple example:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl

use strict;
use warnings;

sub extract_names {
    my ($text) = @_;

    # Match first name followed by a space or last name
    my @names = $text =~ m/(Mr|Mrs|Miss|Ms|Dr|Sir|Prof|(\w+)\s*(\w+))/g;

    # Remove titles and return only names
    return grep { /\w\S*/ } @names;
}

# Test the function with a sample text
my $text = "Mr John Doe, Mrs Jane Smith, Dr Alice Waters, Sir Richard Branson, Prof Stephen Hawking";

print join(", ", extract_names($text)), "\n";
```

This script defines a function `extract_names` that takes a string as input and returns an array of names found in the text. The regular expression used in the function matches common title abbreviations followed by one or more words (first name) and zero or more whitespace characters, followed by another word (last name).

The script then tests the function with a sample text containing names and titles. The output will be:

```
John, Jane, Alice, Richard, Stephen
```

You can save this code in a file named `extract_names.pl` and run it using the command `perl extract_names.pl`. Make sure to have Perl installed on your system.

