 Here's a simple JavaScript function that reverses an array in-place:

```javascript
function reverseArray(arr) {
    let start = 0;
    let end = arr.length - 1;

    while (start < end) {
        // Swap elements at start and end
        [arr[start], arr[end]] = [arr[end], arr[start]];
        start++;
        end--;
    }

    return arr;
}

// Example usage:
const inputArray = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
console.log(reverseArray(inputArray)); // Output: [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]
```

This function uses two pointers, one starting from the beginning of the array and the other from the end. It swaps the elements at the current positions of the pointers, then increments the start pointer and decrements the end pointer until they meet in the middle. The function returns the reversed array, but since it modifies the original array in-place, you can simply assign the result of the function to the original array if you want to keep the changes.

