 Here's a simple recursive function to calculate the nth Fibonacci number in JavaScript:

```javascript
function fibonacci(n) {
  if (n <= 1) return n;
  return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2);
}
```

However, this function has an exponential time complexity due to repeated calculations. To improve performance, we can use dynamic programming (memoization) or an iterative approach:

Memoized version:

```javascript
const fibonacciMemo = (function() {
  const memo = {};

  return function fibonacci(n) {
    if (n in memo) return memo[n];
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    memo[n] = fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2);
    return memo[n];
  };
})();
```

Iterative version:

```javascript
function fibonacciIterative(n) {
  let fib = [0, 1];

  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    fib[i] = fib[i - 1] + fib[i - 2];
  }

  return fib[n];
}
```

Both of these versions have a linear time complexity, making them more efficient for larger inputs.

