}
``
In this function, we iterate through all properties of the given object using a for-in loop. If we find any property (including those inherited from the prototype chain), the function returns false because the object is not empty. If we don't find any properties, the function returns true, indicating that the object is empty.

However, it's worth noting that this function will not work correctly with objects created using Object.create(null) since they don't have any properties (neither own nor inherited). To handle such cases, you can modify the function as follows:

```javascript
function isEmpty(obj) {
    if (obj === null || typeof obj !== 'object') return true;
    if (obj.constructor === Object && Object.getPrototypeOf(obj) === null) return Object.keys(obj).length === 0;
