 In your case, since you're already using HTTPS for communication, which encrypts the data in transit, you're already taking a significant step towards securing your passwords. However, it's worth noting that HTTPS only encrypts the data while it's being transmitted, not when it's stored on the client or server.

If you're storing the passwords on the server, it's a good practice to hash and salt them before storing, rather than storing them in plain text. This way, even if the password database is compromised, the attacker won't be able to easily obtain the original passwords.

For hashing, you can use a well-established algorithm like Argon2, bcrypt, or PBKDF2. These algorithms are designed to be computationally expensive, making them resistant to brute-force attacks.

For salting, you should generate a unique salt for each password. This helps to prevent rainbow table attacks.

As for your MFC application, you can use the Windows Cryptography API to hash and salt your passwords. Here's a simple example of how you might use the BCrypt password-based key derivation function (PBKDF2) to hash a password:

```cpp
#include <windows.h>
#include <string>
#include <iostream>

void HashPassword(const std::string& password, const std::string& salt, std::vector<byte>& hashedPassword)
{
    // Length of the password and salt in bytes
    DWORD passwordLength = static_cast<DWORD>(password.length());
    DWORD saltLength = static_cast<DWORD>(salt.length());

    // Total length of the hashed password
    DWORD hashedPasswordLength = 0;
    DWORD iterations = 1000; // Number of iterations for PBKDF2

    // Allocate memory for the hashed password
    hashedPassword.resize(BCRYPT_GENERIC_HASH_LENGTH);

    // Initialize the BCryptOpenKey call
    HCRYPTPROV hProv;
    if (!BCryptOpenKey(NULL, MS_ENHANCED_KEY_PROV, NULL, &hProv, 0, 0))
    {
        std::cerr << "Error: BCryptOpenKey" << std::endl;
        return;
    }

    // Initialize the BCryptGenerateKey call
    HCRYPTHASH hHash;
    if (!BCryptCreateHash(hProv, CALG_SHA_256, 0, 0, &hHash, 0, 0))
    {
        std::cerr << "Error: BCryptCreateHash" << std::endl;
        BCryptCloseKey(hProv);
        return;
    }

    // Add the password and salt to the hash
    BYTE passwordData[BCRYPT_BLOCK_SIZE];
    BYTE saltData[BCRYPT_BLOCK_SIZE];
    ZeroMemory(passwordData, sizeof(passwordData));
    ZeroMemory(saltData, sizeof(saltData));

    CopyMemory(passwordData, password.c_str(), passwordLength);
    CopyMemory(saltData, salt.c_str(), saltLength);

    if (!BCryptHashData(hHash, passwordData, passwordLength, 0))
    {
        std::cerr << "Error: BCryptHashData" << std::endl;
        BCryptDestroyHash(hHash);
        BCryptCloseKey(hProv);
        return;
    }

    if (!BCryptHashData(hHash, saltData, saltLength, 0))
    {
        std::cerr << "Error: BCryptHashData" << std::endl;
        BCryptDestroyHash(hHash);
        BCryptCloseKey(hProv);
        return;
    }

    // Derive the hashed password
    if (!BCryptDeriveKey(hHash, CALG_SHA_256, iterations, 0, hashedPassword.data(), hashedPasswordLength))
    {
        std::cerr << "Error: BCryptDeriveKey" << std::endl;
        BCryptDestroyHash(hHash);
        BCryptCloseKey(hProv);
        return;
    }

    // Clean up
    BCryptDestroyHash(hHash);
    BCryptCloseKey(hProv);
}
```

This function takes a password and a salt, hashes the password using PBKDF2 with SHA-256, and stores the result in the `hashedPassword` vector. You can adjust the number of iterations to increase the computational cost of the hash.

