 To print a binary tree in level order, you can use a Queue (like ArrayDeque) to store the nodes in each level. Here's a simple implementation of a binary tree node and a method to print the tree in level order:

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

class TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode left;
    TreeNode right;

    TreeNode(int x) {
        val = x;
    }
}

public class BinaryTreeLevelOrder {

    public static void levelOrder(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        Deque<TreeNode> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(root);

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode currentNode = queue.poll();
            System.out.print(currentNode.val + " ");

            if (currentNode.left != null) {
                queue.offer(currentNode.left);
            }

            if (currentNode.right != null) {
                queue.offer(currentNode.right);
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(1);
        root.left = new TreeNode(2);
        root.right = new TreeNode(3);
        root.left.left = new TreeNode(4);
        root.left.right = new TreeNode(5);

        System.out.println("Level Order Traversal:");
        levelOrder(root);
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a `TreeNode` class to represent the binary tree nodes. The `levelOrder` method takes a root node as input and uses a queue to traverse the tree level by level, printing the values of the nodes.

The main method creates a sample binary tree and calls the `levelOrder` method to print the level order traversal.

 Here's a simple Java function that prints out the Fibonacci sequence up to a given number `n`. This function uses an optimization technique called memoization to avoid calculating the same Fibonacci number multiple times.

```java
public class Main {
    private static long[] memo = new long[100];

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 10; // Change this value to print more or fewer Fibonacci numbers
        printFibonacci(n);
    }

    public static void printFibonacci(int n) {
        if (n > memo.length) {
            System.out.println("Error: The given number is too large.");
            return;
        }

        if (memo[0] == 0) {
            memo[0] = 0;
            memo[1] = 1;
        }

        for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
            if (memo[i] == 0) {
                memo[i] = memo[i - 1] + memo[i - 2];
            }
        }

        System.out.print("Fibonacci sequence up to " + n + ": ");
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            System.out.print(memo[i] + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

You can change the value of `n` in the `main` method to print more or fewer Fibonacci numbers. This program will only work for numbers up to 100 due to the size of the `memo` array. If you need to handle larger numbers, you can adjust the size of the `memo` array accordingly.

