 Here's a simple C++ algorithm to find the maximum and minimum elements in an unsorted array. This algorithm uses two variables, `min` and `max`, to keep track of the minimum and maximum values found so far.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

void findMinMax(const std::vector<int>& arr, int& minValue, int& maxValue) {
    if (arr.empty()) {
        std::cout << "Error: The array is empty." << std::endl;
        return;
    }

    minValue = arr[0];
    maxValue = arr[0];

    for (size_t i = 1; i < arr.size(); ++i) {
        if (arr[i] < minValue) {
            minValue = arr[i];
        }
        if (arr[i] > maxValue) {
            maxValue = arr[i];
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> arr = {5, 3, 8, 1, 9, 7, 2, 6};
    int minValue, maxValue;

    findMinMax(arr, minValue, maxValue);

    std::cout << "Minimum value: " << minValue << std::endl;
    std::cout << "Maximum value: " << maxValue << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This code defines a function `findMinMax` that takes an array and initializes the minimum and maximum values to the first element of the array. Then, it iterates through the rest of the array and updates the minimum and maximum values if it finds a smaller or larger value, respectively. The `main` function demonstrates how to use this function with a sample array.

