```

2. Configure the rate limiter in your `SecurityConfiguration` class:

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig extends WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter {

    @Bean
    public RateLimiterRegistry rateLimiterRegistry() {
        return RateLimiterRegistry.create();
    }

    @Bean
    public RateLimiter rateLimiter(RateLimiterRegistry registry) {
        return registry.limitForKey("public-url")
                .permitsPerDay(100) // Set the number of requests allowed per day
                .build();
    }

    // ... other configurations
}
```

In this example, the rate limiter allows 100 requests per day for the key "public-url". You can adjust the number of requests allowed per day or any other time period according to your needs.

3. Apply the rate limiter to your controller methods:

```java
@Controller
public class MyController {

    @Autowired
    private RateLimiter rateLimiter;

    @GetMapping("/public-url")
    public String handlePublicUrl(HttpServletRequest request) {
        rateLimiter.acquire(1); // Acquire one permit
        // Your logic here
    }
}
```

In this example, the `handlePublicUrl` method acquires one permit before processing the request. If the rate limiter is exhausted, the request will be denied.

This implementation should help protect your application against a simple DoS attack by limiting the number of requests from a single IP address in a short period of time. However, it's essential to note that this is a basic rate limiting solution and may not be sufficient for high-traffic applications or more sophisticated attacks. For more advanced protection, consider using dedicated rate limiting services or WAF (Web Application Firewall) solutions.

