client.cpp
boost::asio::ssl::context ssl_context(boost::asio::ssl::context::tls);
ssl_context.set_verify_mode(boost::asio::ssl::verify_peer);

ssl_context.load_verify_file("server.crt");

If I don't add the ssl_context.load_verify_file("server.crt"); line on the client, I get the following errors from both sides during SSL handshake:
server: handshake: tlsv1 alert unknown ca
client: handshake: certificate verify failed
So, is it safe to distribute the "server.crt" certificate chain file with the client? If not, how should this be done instead?

response:
 Distributing the server's certificate file with the client is generally not recommended for security reasons. The certificate file contains the public key of the server, which can be used to encrypt data sent from the client to the server. If an attacker intercepts the certificate file, they could potentially impersonate the server and perform a man-in-the-middle attack.

Instead, you should use a trusted Certificate Authority (CA) to issue a certificate for your server. The client can then download the CA's root certificate and use it to verify the server's certificate during the SSL handshake. This way, the client can be sure that the server's certificate is genuine and has not been tampered with.

Here's an example of how you can modify your code to use a CA-issued certificate:

server.cpp
```cpp
boost::asio::ssl::context ssl_context(boost::asio::ssl::context::tls);

// Load the CA's root certificate
std::ifstream ca_cert_file("ca.crt");
std::stringstream ca_cert_ss;
ca_cert_file >> ca_cert_ss.rdbuf();
boost::asio::io::buffer ca_cert_buffer(ca_cert_ss.data(), ca_cert_ss.size());
ssl_context.set_verify_mode(boost::asio::ssl::verify_peer);
ssl_context.set_verify_callback(
    [](boost::system::error_code& ec,
       const boost::asio::ssl::verify_context& verify_context) {
        if (verify_context.verify_error == boost::asio::ssl::verify_error::none)
            return true;
        if (verify_context.verify_error == boost::asio::ssl::verify_error::self_signed_certificate)
            return true; // Self-signed certificates can be accepted if necessary
        return false;
    }
);

// Use the server's certificate and private key
ssl_context.use_certificate_chain_file("server.crt");
ssl_context.use_private_key_file("server.key", boost::asio::ssl::context::pem);
ssl_context.use_tmp_dh_file("dh2048.pem");
```

client.cpp
```cpp
boost::asio::ssl::context ssl_context(boost::asio::ssl::context::tls);

// Load the CA's root certificate
std::ifstream ca_cert_file("ca.crt");
std::stringstream ca_cert_ss;
ca_cert_file >> ca_cert_ss.rdbuf();
boost::asio::io::buffer ca_cert_buffer(ca_cert_ss.data(), ca_cert_ss.size());

// Load the server's certificate
std::ifstream server_cert_file("server.crt");
std::stringstream server_cert_ss;
server_cert_file >> server_cert_ss.rdbuf();
boost::asio::io::buffer server_cert_buffer(server_cert_ss.data(), server_cert_ss.size());

// Set the server's certificate and the CA's root certificate
ssl_context.set_verify_mode(boost::asio::ssl::verify_peer);
ssl_context.set_verify_callback(
    [](boost::system::error_code& ec,
       const boost::asio::ssl::verify_context& verify_context) {
        if (verify_context.verify_error == boost::asio::ssl::verify_error::none)
            return true;
        if (verify_context.verify_error == boost::asio::ssl::verify_error::self_signed_certificate)
            return true; // Self-signed certificates can be accepted if necessary
        return false;
    }
);
ssl_context.load_verify_file(nullptr);
ssl_context.use_certificate(server_cert_buffer);
ssl_context.use_tmp_dh_file("dh2048.pem");
```

In this example, the client loads the CA's root certificate and the server's certificate, and uses them to verify the server's certificate during the SSL handshake. The server's certificate is not distributed with the client, but instead downloaded by the client before the SSL handshake. This way, the server's certificate can be securely verified without distributing it with the client.

